manufacturers and display designers frequently seek new ways to provide useful information to drivers while lowering the mental demands associated with processing information. Positioning visual information closer to drivers’ head-up, useful field of view (e.g., on or through the windshield of a vehicle) is one method of improving driving performance and lowering mental workload [2]. The expectation that driving performance can be improved using windshield displays has resulted in a higher interest in integrating information directly onto a windshield.
Augmented reality HUDs offer a promising mechanism to support this type of information integration possible. Augmented Reality (AR) blends the real world with virtual, computer-generated graphics resulting in the ability to place visual information and cues directly into users’ line of sight [3]. Using graphics such as text, symbols or outlines allows users to experience the real world with digital enhancements that may improve their ability to perform tasks or gather information, such as identifying pedestrians or roadway hazards while driving.
Visual User Interface Design In a vehicle, secondary information can be provided to drivers in many ways, two of which are in the form of headup or head-down displays. A head-up display (HUD) is a display that provides information via digital graphics close to the driver’s natural line of sight. A head-down display (HDD), in contrast, is a display which provides information below the driver’s line of sight, usually on the dashboard or the middle console in a location which forces the driver to divert their visual attention away from the road [4]. AR HUDs have been shown to help keep drivers’ eyes on the road with lower levels of mental workload, and consequently improved driving performance [5].
While AR HUDs are widely used in military aircraft allowing for easy access to flight information, navigation systems, and target locating [3], care must be taking in designing head-up user interfaces since visual complexity can dramatically affect the usability of HUDs [6]. Generally speaking, aircrafts have less visual background complexity due to environmental surroundings and fewer objects to interact with as compared to automobile settings. Incorporating HUD systems within ground vehicles is potentially subject to more risk due to highly dynamic traffic conditions and complex roadway navigation. In addition, there is debate as to whether adding graphics to the windshield will be beneficial or simply more distracting [7]. Finally, it is worth recognizing that the driver population is considerably more variable (in

