perceptual/cognitive abilities, expectations, motivations, etc.) compared to highly trained/regulated pilots.
While HUDs remove the need for a user to shift their gaze away from the road, AR images may change individuals’ cognitive responses. Early research suggests that people cannot process the real world background and an AR graphic concurrently, as the mind has to treat them as two separate images [8]. Thus, simply overlaying information onto the real world can result in additional processing to, at a minimum, correctly identify what is real and what is computer generated, thus increasing the workload [9]. In driving scenarios, keeping eyes on the road may provide many benefits but the tradeoff of increased mental workload due to the need to parse out the real world from the digital may result in serious consequences.
One study of commercial drivers in Taiwan using HUDs and HDDs showed a decrease in response time to unexpected events and more consistent vehicle speed control, but no difference in average accuracy rate or mean vehicle speed when using a HUD [4]. Kerr’s walking navigation study showed negative effects `on performance when using AR displays [10]. For example, participants struggled to notice oncoming traffic when crossing roads or sidewalk trip hazards in Kerr’s study. In addition, Kerr’s participants frequently reported difficulty reading text due to bright light outdoors. At times, AR HUDs can improve performance, while at other times they clearly cause problems.
Task Design The nature and structure of tasks must also be considered in analyzing HUDs. One type of secondary task that a driver might perform is a structured visual search task, such as reading a document or navigating a list arranged in alphabetical order. A user’s search pattern follows a predictable path. Because of the nature of these tasks, it may be difficult to resume these tasks after interruptions. Individuals must be able to remember and located their exact spot after interruptions to be able to resume the task, so interruptions may result in re-starting the task or degraded performance.
An unstructured visual search task, in contrast, creates a visual field where the user’s eye is not expected to follow one expected or set path during their visual search. The target might be positioned anywhere in the field, and does not have to stay on a line or grid pattern. For example, drivers should constantly scan the vehicle path for roadway hazards without any expectation of where one might occur.
Often, real world tasks are semi-structured, that is a hybrid of the structured and unstructured tasks. For example, a structured grid could be used to display visual cues, but the users would not have to search in the left-to-right, line-byline method that is typical in structured visual search text tasks. In a vehicle, display lights can come on at any time (indicating low fuel, check engine, etc.), but there is a finite set of spaces where these lights can occur. Another example is the grid pattern of icons seen frequently in touch screen displays, such as smart phones. Semi-structured tasks are

typically easier to resume after interruptions, because locations can be generally remembered, rather than an exact location. Drivers perform a variety of search tasks, structured, unstructured, or a hybrid of the two, and it is important to understand how HUDs and HDDs affect different types of search tasks. While this study used an AR HUD specifically, the tasks were designed to be similar to those used in standard HUD displays.
Before AR HUD technology is used widely in ground vehicles, we must determine the differences between AR HUD displays and current technology. While keeping eyes on the road is an important factor, if AR HUD information is provided in a sub-optimal method, it may lead to confusion and further driver distraction. Using AR HUDs may provide benefits by keeping drivers’ eyes on the road but there may also be hidden problems associated with adding more information to an increasingly complex system. This study examines the possible effects of using HUDs as compared to HDDs using two types of visual search tasks.
METHODS
Participants Sixteen gender-balanced participants completed the experiment. The participants were 21 to 52 years old with previous driving experience in the United Kingdom and perfect or corrected vision (men: mean age = 28, SD = 5.2; women: mean age = 33, SD = 12.3).
Materials The driving simulator was a fixed-base right-hand-drive vehicle. We used STISIM Version 2 software and a curved large screen projection to provide visual cues to drivers. A Pioneer HUD with a focal depth of about 3 meters was placed just above the driver’s line of vision, roughly where a sun visor would be placed (Figure 1).
