Primary Task The primary task of the participant was to drive a simulator vehicle on a standard UK motorway with 3 lanes in each direction, while following a lead car. The lead car fluctuated in speed from 65 to 75 MPH. Each participant completed the primary task six different times, including one familiarization drive, one baseline drive, and four different driving scenarios in which they also performed a secondary visual search task (described below). Both degraded performance and more conservative driving habits are indicative of an increase in mental workload [11].

Two types of metrics are commonly used in the evaluation of driving performance: longitudinal control and lateral control. Longitudinal control is evaluated according to SAE J2944 standards by measuring time to collision, distance or time gap, distance or time headway, and other similar measures. Lateral lane position, measured in feet, is also used to assess driving performance [12]. For the purposes of this study, lateral lane position was defined according to SAE J2944 10.1.1.3 (Option C). The participants drove on a flat and straight road, diminishing common concerns with using Option C in lane position evaluation of driving simulators. The Standard Deviation of Lane Position (SDLP) as defined in SAE J2944 10.1.3 was used for all SDLP calculations (Equation 1).

