Secondary Visual Search Tasks Throughout the study, participants completed two different types of secondary tasks: structured and semi-structured visual search tasks. We visually matched the color and size of text between the two displays to ensure that the images were as close as possible. We changed the color of the

82

HDD graphics to mimic the slight green tint of the HUD. Because the matching was done visually rather than strictly controlling for size, the font and colors may have been slightly different. Structured Visual Search Task (Text): For this study, pseudo text was used in place of familiar words in order to avoid influence from a user’s reading pace or previous knowledge of words or context. The pseudo text is built from a randomized sequence of letters, both capitalized and lowercase, spaced similarly to written words but following ISO 9241-3 [13]. To complete the visual search task, a participant scans this pseudo text to locate and count the occurrences of a pre-determined target letter ‘E’ (Figure 3). Each display had at least one target letter and no more than six. There were three levels of task complexity with 2, 4, or 6 rows of text for low, medium, and high difficulty tasks, respectively. After reading through the text, participants verbally stated the number of target letters present in that specific set of pseudo text.
Figure 4: This pseudo-text visual search task forces bottom-up processing, requiring participants to focus on each letter to locate and count targets (capital ‘E’). The correct answer for this set is 1.
Semi-structured Search Task (Grid): To conceptually simulate a 2D indicator/control layout (e.g., that could be used in center console display), we developed a square grid layout where participants would need to locate a set of target letters (i.e., ‘AC’) within a n x n pattern of visual stimuli.
Figure 5: HDD Semi-Structured Visual Search Task, 4x4 Grid. Due to the design of the HUD, the text had a green tint
to it. While a left-to-right reading pattern could be followed, the grid structure of the visual task allowed for a variety of eye movements. The location of the target AC within the grid was randomized. Each grid cell contained two-letter combinations selected from a pool of potential distractors either beginning with ‘A’ or ending in ‘C’. No real words

(e.g. AT, AM) were used in the grid. To complete the task, participants had to first locate, then verbally state the row location of the single target. We used 3x3, 4x4, and 5x5 grids for low, medium, and high task complexity (respectively).
Procedure Prior to any driving, participants familiarized themselves with the displays and secondary tasks. They then completed the practice and baseline drives. Participants experienced the four secondary task conditions one at a time in a counterbalanced order. At the beginning of each drive, participants began by engaging in the primary task of following the specified car. Once underway, we introduced the secondary task demands. Each participant completed 15 trials for each condition (3 levels of complexity x 5 replications) with a random interval between 10-15 seconds of driving time between each trial. To indicate completion of the trial (counting the ‘E’ targets for the text condition or stating the row number of the ‘AC’ target in the grid condition), participants verbally stated the answer, and subsequently reported their confidence in performing the secondary task (ranging from 1, lowest confidence, to 10, highest confidence). Participants stopped the car after completing the last trial.
We recorded each answer and confidence level, and later manually calculated the number of errors for each condition. As soon as a participant stated the number or location of targets, we advanced to the next slide. The custom Visual Basic for Applications software logged these times, which we then used to determine the amount of time required to complete each task. We tracked participant eye movements such as glance number, glance length, and fixation time using the SMI eye tracking suite. For the primary task, speed and lane position were captured by the STISIM driving software. After completing all drives, we asked participants which display they preferred for legibility, ease of use, convenience, and overall. They were then asked to complete NASA-TLX rate 6 components of workload under each condition.
ANALYSIS For this paper, we report on dependent measures related to primary task (driving) performance (lane position), secondary task performance (task response time and error) and participant preferences related to the displays.
Driving Performance Data While we have collected a suite of longitudinal data, we have not yet examined SAE-recommended longitudinal data such as time to collision, distance to vehicle ahead, distance gap, time gap, etc., but did an initial look at speed and standard deviation of speed. We excluded the first 2,000 feet of driving distance because this was the space required to merge onto the road, steady the car, and begin to follow the lead car. All data after participants slowed down from 4th gear for the last time was also excluded, as this indicated that they had finished the secondary task and were instructed to stop the vehicle to end that condition.

