DISCUSSION This study paints an initial picture of driver performance and preference considerations when designing for in-vehicle HDD and HUD displays. While HUDs offer some potential benefits such as perceived increased ease of use and convenience for drivers, they may result in compromised driving performance such as an increase in errors for specific secondary tasks.
Task Design We expected that the text visual search task performance would suffer as participants would find it difficult to resume this task after interruption (i.e. looking back at the road to ensure safe driving). The higher number of errors, lower confidence levels, and higher reported mental load all support this expectation. Some of the differences in errors, confidence, and mental load may also be related to inherent task difficulty. Conversely, the grid visual search task, which was designed to better support resumability, scored higher in each of these areas.
In addition, the text visual search tasks were associated with higher standard deviations of lane position as compared to the grid visual search tasks and the control. This indicates that there was more visual distraction in the text tasks [16].

