Task design also affected the type of lessons learned that can be gleaned from our study. The difference in error rates between this study and Liu’s [4] may be due to the nature of the tasks performed. Liu’s experiment used commercial goods delivery professionals and incorporated secondary tasks that were directly related to the driving and delivery tasks. Our study sought to understand how adding cognitive load via HDD and HUD would affect driving and secondary task performance on ordinary drivers when participants could not use logic and context to infer likely answers. Instead, our secondary tasks purposefully examined bottom-up cognitive processing to provide a glimpse at the basic level on the effect of adding various cognitive loads might have on drivers.
Visual User Interface Design Even with the potential benefits of HUDs, moving forward we should be careful in designing information displays for drivers because some HUD interface designs may be associated with greater degrees of lane drift than others. Therefore, the design of the information flow (i.e. top-down vs. bottom-up) should be carefully considered in conjunction with display type. For example, Tables 1 and 2 suggest that a text visual search task (bottom-up) may be associated with greater lane drift than grid-based visual search tasks (top-down). What are the effects of other types of visual information processing tasks on lane drift?
People may feel more comfortable using a HUD, as evidenced by the higher confidence levels and preference scores observed. This is congruent with Kerr’s findings that participants were unable to effectively read text, but that they generally liked using the AR devices [10]. Our results suggest that comfort and tendency to accept new forms of technology are not directly associated with improved performance. This conflict in preference and performance is often cited in usability studies (e.g., [17]). In this instance, both driving performance and secondary task accuracy were worse in HUD-text conditions, even though participants generally preferred the HUD and reported no increase in mental workload in these conditions. Importantly, our study provides evidence that individual preferences such as willingness to readily adopt new technology might predispose drivers to use HUDs even though it may diminish their driving abilities.
Furthermore, the legibility problems noted by participants pose some concern in the HUD condition. The text condition had less space between text, which we think is closely connected to the difference between legibility preferences of the HUD and HDD conditions. The degradations in performance (errors, speed) in the HUDtext condition may be due to the text being hard to read. Using a more legible text could have resulted in speed improvement with similar or fewer errors to the HDD-text condition.
In addition, see-through AR HUDs are typically subject to color blending [18] which can affect the overall usability of

the display. Increasing the brightness of a display can create richer looking colors and minimize blending, but may in turn wash out the display images [19] and/or occlude important real-world background visual cues. We believe the HDD display was considered more legible because of this washing out of the display image in the HUD condition. However, even with a clearly superior HDD, participants preferred the HUD overall. Participants were biased in favor of new technology regardless of its effect on their performance.
CONCLUSION & FUTURE WORK While some task designs (i.e. structured, non-resumable) are not well-supported by HUDs, people seem predisposed to adopting this technology without realizing how much it distracts them from the driving task. Performance may be affected even though the images are displayed within the line of sight. Other displays of information, such as grid patterns that do not afford an obvious visual search pattern, but support resumability, may be better suited for HUD presentation (assuming that the interface maintains a reasonable level of visual complexity).
Display designers need to carefully consider the high acceptance of HUDs due to novelty before implementing interface designs, and they should develop interfaces that that have been proven to support resumability. Because people perceive that they are still paying attention to the primary task of driving, HUD tasks that do not support resumability (and therefore encourage drivers to focus on the display for longer periods of time) may cause even bigger problems than HDD interfaces. In this case, an interface that cannot support resumability should not be incorporated into HUDs at all.
Future work will re-evaluate this data considering the longitudinal measures of driving data according the SAE standards. In addition, we will examine the data collected about eye-tracking to understand more about the visual attention of drivers when using HUDs and HDDs in various types of visual search tasks. Task complexity with relation to display type should also be examined. Further studies must be performed in order to understand the intricacies of the interactions between display type and the information or images presented to drivers.
